1.  Introduction

   vCard 4.0 provides a standardized format for representing personal identity information. This document introduces a set of extensions tailored for decentralized social identity ecosystems, where users maintain control over their identity by self-hosting their vCard profiles. These extensions enable vCards to specify canonical sources, advertise feed endpoints, and include social-media identifiers in a structured, interoperable, and machine-readable format.

   Profile feeds, specified using the `X-FEED` property, allow users to associate RSS or Atom feeds with their vCard profiles. These feeds can provide updates, posts, or other activity streams, enabling seamless integration with decentralized social networks. Each feed can include optional attributes such as `LANGUAGE` for localization and `TAGS` for content classification, ensuring flexibility and relevance for diverse use cases.

   By leveraging the "X-" prefix for all custom properties, as allowed by the vCard specification, this approach ensures compatibility with existing systems while supporting extensibility for future decentralized use cases.


2.  Conventions Used in This Document

   The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "SHOULD", and "MAY" are to be
   interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

   All examples use vCard 4.0 syntax as defined in RFC 6350.


3. Identity

   The profile is declared using a vCard 4.0.

3.1. Required Properties

3.1.1.  SOURCE

   The vCard MUST include a SOURCE property containing the canonical URL
   from which the vCard can be retrieved.

   Requirements:

   *  The value MUST be an absolute URL.
   *  The URL SHOULD use HTTPS.
   *  The URL SHOULD dereference to the vCard itself.

   Example:

     SOURCE:https://example.com/alice.vcf


3.2. Verification

   Identities MAY include their public key in the vCard using:

     KEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;ENCODING=b:<BASE64_DER_KEY>

   Implementations MUST support Ed25519 keys.  Additional algorithms MAY
   be supported.

   A vCard MAY contain multiple `KEY` entries.  The preferred key SHOULD
   be marked with `PREF=1`.

   Throughout this document, the "Key-ID" of a key is the exact value of
   its `KEY` property: the base64 encoding of the DER-encoded
   SubjectPublicKeyInfo of the public key (`<BASE64_DER_KEY>`).
   `<KEY_ID>` and `<ENDORSEE_KEY_B64>` are alternative names for this
   same value.  For Ed25519, the DER encoding is 44 bytes.

3.2.1. Key Revocation

3.2.1.1.  REVKEY Property

   Revocations MUST be expressed using:

     REVKEY[;REASON=<reason>][;DATE=<timestamp>];ENCODING=b:<KEY_ID>

   The `DATE` parameter SHOULD be present and MUST use
   `YYYYMMDDThhmmssZ` format.


3.2.1.2.  Revocation Reasons

   The following reasons are defined:

   * compromised  
       The private key is believed to be stolen or exposed.

   * rotated  
       The key has been intentionally replaced with a new one.

   * superseded  
       The key is replaced due to algorithm migration or identity
       restructuring.

   * retired  
       The key is no longer used but not compromised.

   * lost  
       The private key is lost and cannot be used.

   * deprecated  
       The key remains valid but SHOULD NOT be used going forward.


3.2.1.3.  Client Behavior for Each Reason

   Clients MUST interpret revocation reasons as follows:

   +-------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+
   | Reason      | Accept old signatures?       | Accept new signatures?       |
   +-------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+
   | compromised | NO                           | NO                           |
   | rotated     | YES (before DATE)            | NO                           |
   | superseded  | YES                           | NO                           |
   | retired     | YES                           | NO                           |
   | lost        | YES                           | NO                           |
   | deprecated  | YES                           | YES (with warning)           |
   +-------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+

   If `DATE` is omitted, clients SHOULD treat the revocation as
   immediate.

3.2.2. Key Rotation
   
   A vCard MAY contain multiple `REVKEY` entries that are used to revoke
   previous keys for a grace period using:

      REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=<REASON>;DATE=<DATE>;ENCODING=b:<BASE64_DER_KEY>
      
      
   <DATE> in format: YYYYMMDDThhmmssZ

3.2.2.1  Rotation Procedure

   To rotate a key, a publisher MUST:

   1. Generate a new keypair.
   2. Add the new public key as a `KEY` property.
   3. Mark the new key with `PREF=1`.
   4. Remove `PREF` from all other keys.
   5. Add a `REVKEY` entry for the old key with reason `rotated`.

   Example:

     KEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;PREF=1;ENCODING=b:<NEW_KEY>
     REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=rotated;DATE=20260210T120000Z;ENCODING=b:<OLD_KEY>


3.2.2.2.  Client Behavior During Rotation

   Clients MUST:

   * Treat the new `PREF=1` key as the active signing key.
   * Continue to accept signatures made by the old key prior to the
     rotation date.
   * Reject new signatures made by the old key after rotation.
   
   
   The rest could be rotated keys and they
   SHOULD NOT be trusted; a REVKEY property SHOULD be used to indicate the
   revocation reason that can be compromised or rotated.

   The rotated keys SHOULD be maintained in the vCard for a grace period of
   30 days after the REVKEY `DATE`, after which publishers MAY remove them.
   Regardless of whether the old key is still present, clients MUST continue
   to apply the DATE-based accept/reject rules defined in Section 3.2.1.3.

   The identity verification can be used to claim authority of the
   spec extensions as feeds or web-trust endorsements
   (see related spec).

3.2.2.3 Examples

   The following properties are examples of keys in the vCard:

   KEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;PREF=1;ENCODING=b:<BASE64_DER_KEY>
   REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=compromised;DATE=20260217T143717Z;ENCODING=b:<BASE64_DER_KEY>
   REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=rotated;DATE=20260221T110230Z;ENCODING=b:<BASE64_DER_KEY>

3.3.  Reserved Extension Properties

3.3.1.  X-FEED

   The X-FEED property defines a URL for an RSS or Atom feed associated
   with the identity.

   Requirements:

   *  The value MUST be an absolute URL.
   *  Multiple X-FEED properties MAY be present.

   Example:

     X-FEED:https://example.com/alice/feed.xml

3.3.1.1. Attributes

   The X-FEED property can have the following attributes:

3.3.1.1.1. LANGUAGE

   The X-FEED property MAY include a LANGUAGE parameter indicating the
   language or locale of the feed.

   Requirements:

   *  The LANGUAGE parameter MUST follow BCP 47 syntax.
   *  Multiple language-specific feeds MAY be present.

   Example:

     X-FEED;LANGUAGE=en-US:https://example.com/alice/en/feed.xml

3.3.1.1.2. TAGS

   The X-FEED property MAY include a TAGS parameter indicating the
   tags of the feed.

   Requirements:

   *  The TAGS parameter MUST be a comma-separated list of lowercase
      alphanumeric tokens.
   *  Multiple tagged feeds MAY be present.

   Example:

     X-FEED;TAGS=nature:https://example.com/alice/en/feed-nature.xml

3.3.2 X-ENDORSE

   The X-ENDORSE property defines the endorsement of other identities
   in the network.


3.3.2.1  X-ENDORSE Property

   Endorsements MUST be represented using a single vCard property:

     X-ENDORSE;SIG=<HEX_SIGNATURE>[;DATE=<timestamp>][;CONFIDENCE=<level>];ENCODING=b:<ENDORSEE_KEY_B64>


   * `<ENDORSEE_KEY_B64>` is the Key-ID of the endorsed key (see
     Section 3.2).
   * `SIG` contains the endorsement signature.
   * `DATE` is an optional timestamp in `YYYYMMDDThhmmssZ`.
   * `CONFIDENCE` is an optional hint (`low`, `medium`, `high`).

   Each endorsement MUST appear as a separate `X-ENDORSE` property.


3.3.2.2.  Canonical Endorsement String

   The canonical string for an endorsement MUST be:

     endorse:<ENDORSEE_KEY_B64>

   The string MUST be encoded in UTF‑8.


3.3.2.3.  Signature Algorithm

   Endorsements MUST be signed using Ed25519.

   The signature MUST be encoded as lowercase hexadecimal (128
   characters for Ed25519) and placed in the `SIG` parameter.


3.3.2.4.  Client Verification

   For each `X-ENDORSE` entry, clients MUST:

   1. Extract `<ENDORSEE_KEY_B64>` from the property value.
   2. Extract the `SIG` parameter.
   3. Reconstruct the canonical string.
   4. Verify the signature using the endorser’s public key.

   Invalid endorsements MUST be ignored.


3.3.2.5.  Interaction with Key Rotation and Revocation

   If an endorsee rotates their key, they SHOULD endorse the new key.
   Trusted endorsers MAY also endorse the new key.

   If a key is revoked, endorsements for that key MUST NOT be considered
   valid after the revocation date.  If the revocation reason is
   `compromised`, clients SHOULD treat all endorsements for that key as
   invalid.

3.3.3.  X-DSI-VERSION

   The X-DSI-VERSION property allows a vCard to declare which draft
   revision and optional features of this specification it implements,
   enabling capability negotiation between publishers and clients.  It
   is distinct from the standard vCard `VERSION` property (Section 7),
   which always identifies the vCard format version (`4.0`).

   Requirements:

   *  The value MUST be the two-digit draft revision identifier the
      vCard was authored against (e.g. `00`, `01`).
   *  The property MAY include a `FEATURES` parameter listing a
      comma-separated set of optional feature tokens the vCard uses
      (e.g. `tags`, `endorse`).
   *  At most one `X-DSI-VERSION` property SHOULD be present.
   *  Clients MUST treat a missing `X-DSI-VERSION` property as
      revision `00` with no optional features declared, for backward
      compatibility.

   Example:

     X-DSI-VERSION;FEATURES=tags,endorse:01

3.4.  Social Identity Extension Properties

3.4.1.  Platform-Specific Properties

   Platform-specific identifiers are represented using custom properties
   of the form:

     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=<PLATFORM>:<value>

   Requirements:

   *  <PLATFORM> MUST consist of lowercase letters a–z.
   *  The value MAY be a username or a full profile URL.
   *  Multiple properties for the same platform MAY be present.

   Examples:

     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=github:alice
     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=reddit:u/alice
     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=discord:alice#1234
     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=bluesky:https://bsky.app/profile/alice.bsky.social
     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=activitypub:https://social.example/@alice
     X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=mastodon:@alice@social.example

   For platforms whose profiles are addressable by URL (e.g.
   `activitypub`), publishers SHOULD use the full profile URL.  For
   other platforms, a platform-specific username or handle is
   RECOMMENDED.

4. Feeds

The feeds represent the latest posts of the identity.  A feed MAY be an
RSS or an Atom document (see Section 3.3.1).  Item signing, as
specified in this section, is defined only for RSS 2.0 `<item>`
elements in this revision; signing of Atom entries is out of scope.

4.1. Authority

   The publishers can optionally sign individual RSS items to provide
   authenticity and integrity guarantees using a key defined in the
   vCard.

4.2. Feed Signing

4.2.1.  Canonical Item String

   Each RSS `<item>` MUST be signed individually.

   The canonical string MUST be constructed as:

     <pubDate>\n<title>\n<description_plain>

   * `<pubDate>` MUST match the exact string in the RSS item.
   * `<title>` MUST be the unescaped text.
   * `<description_plain>` MUST be the plain-text description before
     HTML or CDATA encoding.

   The canonical string MUST use UTF‑8 encoding.


4.2.2.  Signature Algorithm

   Feed signatures MUST use Ed25519.

   The signature MUST be encoded as lowercase hexadecimal.


4.2.3.  Feed Item Format

   Each signed item MUST include:

```xml
<signature alg="ed25519" key-id="<KEY_ID>">HEX_SIGNATURE</signature>
```

   * `alg` MUST match the algorithm in the vCard.
   * `key-id` SHOULD match the Key-ID of the signing key.


4.2.4.  Client Verification

   For each feed item, clients MUST:

   1. Extract the signature and `key-id`.
   2. Retrieve the corresponding public key from the publisher’s vCard.
   3. Reconstruct the canonical string.
   4. Verify the signature.

   Items with invalid signatures MUST be treated as untrusted.


5.  Processing Requirements

5.1.  Client Requirements

   Clients implementing this specification MUST:

   *  Retrieve the vCard from the URL in the SOURCE property.
   *  When a vCard was retrieved from a URL, compare its `SOURCE` value
      with the requested URL, after normalization (case-insensitive
      scheme and host, removal of a default port and of a trailing dot
      in the host).  If they do not match, the client MUST NOT treat the
      vCard as the identity published at the requested URL (see
      Section 8).
   *  Preserve unknown X- properties when reserializing the vCard.
   *  Recognize X-FEED as feed endpoints.
   *  Recognize KEY as identity verification.
   *  Recognize X-ENDORSE as identity endorsements.
   *  Recognize X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=<PLATFORM> properties as social identifiers.
   *  Recognize X-DSI-VERSION as the declared draft revision and feature set.

5.2.  Client Recommendations

   Clients implementing this specification SHOULD:

   *  Prefer language-specific feeds when matching user locale.
   *  Normalize usernames where platform conventions allow.
   *  Display platform icons or labels based on property names.

5.3.  Error Handling

   Clients MUST NOT fail to process an otherwise well-formed vCard
   solely because of a malformed DSI extension property.  Specifically,
   clients MUST:

   *  Ignore a `SOURCE` property whose value is not an absolute URL,
      and MUST NOT treat the vCard as self-authoritative for that
      property.
   *  Treat an `X-FEED` URL that cannot be dereferenced, times out, or
      returns a non-2xx status as unavailable, and MUST NOT fail
      processing of the rest of the vCard.
   *  Ignore any `KEY` or `REVKEY` property whose parameter set is
      malformed (e.g. missing `ENCODING`, an unrecognized `ALG`, or a
      `DATE` that does not match `YYYYMMDDThhmmssZ`), and MUST NOT
      treat the associated key as valid or revoked.
   *  Log or otherwise surface ignored properties for debugging
      purposes, without exposing them as trusted data to the user.


6.  Extensibility

   This specification reserves the following `X-` properties.  Clients
   MUST preserve any unrecognized `X-` property when reserializing a
   vCard (see Section 5.1), so unregistered extensions remain
   forward-compatible.

   +----------------+-----------------------------------------------+
   | Property       | Purpose                                        |
   +----------------+-----------------------------------------------+
   | X-FEED         | RSS/Atom feed endpoint (Section 3.3.1)         |
   | X-ENDORSE      | Identity endorsement (Section 3.3.2)           |
   | X-DSI-VERSION  | Draft revision / feature negotiation           |
   |                | (Section 3.3.3)                                |
   | X-SOCIAL       | Platform-specific identifier (Section 3.4.1)   |
   +----------------+-----------------------------------------------+

   Additional platforms MAY be represented using new
   X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=<PLATFORM> properties without requiring
   registration.  Communities MAY maintain shared, informally-governed
   lists of recommended `PLATFORM` values (e.g. `github`, `reddit`,
   `discord`, `bluesky`, `activitypub`, `mastodon`) to reduce accidental collisions between
   unrelated platforms choosing the same token.

   New top-level `X-` properties beyond those listed above SHOULD be
   distinguished with a vendor- or project-specific prefix (e.g.
   `X-ACME-FOO`) to avoid colliding with a future revision of this
   specification or with another implementation's extension.


7.  Example vCard

   The following example illustrates a complete DSI‑vCard:

   BEGIN:VCARD
   VERSION:4.0
   FN:Alice Example
   N:Example;Alice;;;
   NICKNAME:Ali
   BDAY:1990-05-15
   EMAIL:alice@example.com
   TEL;TYPE=cell:+1234567890
   ADR;TYPE=home:;;123 Main Street;Hometown;CA;12345;USA
   KEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;PREF=1;ENCODING=b:MCowBQYDK2VwAyEAOAiOTCroL1xFxoCKYaZJDTxhLOHaI1cURm/HSPvEy7s=
   REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=compromised;DATE=20260217T143717Z;ENCODING=b:MCowBQYDK2VwAyEA0YGzEO2QT50oIZ71H2TcJ/D1LmMVlLmX5Cy5aQcwPOw=
   REVKEY;TYPE=public;ALG=ed25519;REASON=rotated;DATE=20260221T110230Z;ENCODING=b:MCowBQYDK2VwAyEAlUfscKrwDEj+S+31tJW+mMalKSEt8XvIxvScw7ou858=
   URL:https://example.com
   SOURCE:https://example.com/alice.vcf
   X-DSI-VERSION;FEATURES=tags,endorse:01
   X-FEED:https://example.com/alice/feed.xml
   X-FEED;LANGUAGE=en-US:https://example.com/alice/en/feed.xml
   X-FEED;LANGUAGE=es-ES:https://example.com/alice/es/feed.xml
   X-FEED;LANGUAGE=en-US;TAGS=nature:https://example.com/alice/en/feed-nature.xml
   X-FEED;LANGUAGE=es-ES;TAGS=nature:https://example.com/alice/es/feed-nature.xml
   X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=github:alice
   X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=reddit:u/alice
   X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=discord:alice#1234
   X-SOCIAL;PLATFORM=bluesky:https://bsky.app/profile/alice.bsky.social
   X-ENDORSE;SIG=0c036138c11d467ad6518502d45dd0eeea4745b17ebea8dac01849cdd3946d679a8c6bd039caff7077abfbc2aff9a1d96bff16b20134c1368fc93a2b17f07e00;DATE=20260210T120000Z;CONFIDENCE=high;ENCODING=b:MCowBQYDK2VwAyEA3XVgQP3VFF4r+YMtJk3QgOSz5zAWvfZXS0zYfqppf14=
   X-ENDORSE;SIG=ee7d3dbc37bc9b9463fd64fea7e05eb27d900967b4af64de0f39b2cf0e4546768c0320a718adccdde09ecd2807230ccba75eea6dd34081ad9255f3fe79cd9d07;DATE=20260211T090000Z;CONFIDENCE=medium;ENCODING=b:MCowBQYDK2VwAyEAPuZ2QmBwf82HjQ+DfemTwqHYrA/Wqg+qE4+HZyKANwM=
   END:VCARD


8.  Security Considerations

   Implementations should consider the authenticity of the SOURCE URL
   and the integrity.

   Identity Substitution:

   *  A vCard served at one URL can declare a different `SOURCE`.
      Clients MUST apply the `SOURCE` comparison defined in
      Section 5.1; otherwise an attacker could present a profile
      belonging to another identity as if it were published at the
      requested URL.

   Transport Security:

   *  Clients SHOULD reject a `SOURCE`, `X-FEED`, or endorsement-
      related fetch over plain HTTP once an HTTPS URL has been seen
      for the same resource, to resist downgrade attacks.
   *  Clients MUST validate TLS certificates when dereferencing
      `SOURCE` and `X-FEED` URLs; certificate validation MUST NOT be
      disabled to work around expired or self-signed certificates.

   Denial of Service and Replay:

   *  A vCard MAY contain an unbounded number of `X-FEED`, `X-ENDORSE`,
      and `REVKEY` entries.  Clients SHOULD impose a reasonable cap on
      how many entries they process or fetch per vCard, and SHOULD
      paginate or lazily load beyond that cap, to avoid resource
      exhaustion from a maliciously large profile.
   *  Clients SHOULD cache verification results (e.g. signature
      checks) to avoid repeated expensive cryptographic operations
      when a vCard or feed is re-fetched unchanged.

   Key Storage:

   *  Publishers MUST protect private keys corresponding to any `KEY`
      property, using platform-appropriate secure storage (e.g. an
      OS keychain, hardware security module, or encrypted-at-rest
      storage).  Private keys MUST NOT be embedded in the vCard or
      any other publicly distributed artifact.

   Clients MUST clearly indicate verification status.
   Implementations SHOULD support key rotation and revocation.
   Endorsements express social trust and do not guarantee identity
   correctness.


9.  Privacy Considerations

   A DSI vCard is designed to be published and retrieved publicly;
   implementers and publishers SHOULD understand the privacy
   implications of doing so.

   *  `X-ENDORSE` and `X-SOCIAL` properties are inherently public and
      MAY be used to correlate an identity across platforms or to
      build a social graph.  Publishers who wish to limit
      cross-platform correlation SHOULD avoid publishing identifiers
      for platforms they do not want linked.
   *  Endorsement graphs (Section 3.3.2) reveal social relationships.
      Publishers SHOULD be aware that endorsing another identity is
      itself a public disclosure of association.
   *  Feed access via `X-FEED` MAY leak metadata (e.g. IP address,
      request timing) to the feed host on every fetch.  Clients SHOULD
      document this to users where feeds are fetched automatically.
   *  Revocation records (`REVKEY`) are retained in the vCard for the
      grace period defined in Section 3.2.2.2 and MAY remain
      discoverable afterward via cached copies, archives, or client
      history; publishers SHOULD NOT rely on removal from the current
      vCard alone to make a compromised key unlinkable from the
      identity.
   *  This specification does not define an access-control mechanism.
      Any information a publisher does not want public MUST NOT be
      placed in a DSI vCard or any resource it references.


Appendix A.  Formal Syntax (ABNF)

   The following defines the syntax of DSI vCard extension properties
   using ABNF (RFC 5234).  Core rules ALPHA, DIGIT, HEXDIG, and CRLF
   are as defined in Appendix B of RFC 5234.  `value-char` follows the
   general vCard property syntax of RFC 6350 Section 3.3.

   ; -- SOURCE --

   SOURCE-prop     = "SOURCE" ":" absolute-URI CRLF

   ; -- KEY --

   KEY-prop        = "KEY" *(";" key-param) ":" b64-key CRLF
   key-param       = pref-param / type-param / alg-param /
                     encoding-param
   pref-param      = "PREF=1"
   type-param      = "TYPE=public"
   alg-param       = "ALG=" alg-name
   alg-name        = "ed25519"                ; only value defined in -00
   encoding-param  = "ENCODING=b"

   ; -- REVKEY --

   REVKEY-prop     = "REVKEY" *(";" revkey-param) ":" b64-key CRLF
   revkey-param    = key-param / reason-param / date-param
   reason-param    = "REASON=" reason
   reason          = "compromised" / "rotated" / "superseded" /
                     "retired" / "lost" / "deprecated"
   date-param      = "DATE=" date-time
   date-time       = 8DIGIT "T" 6DIGIT "Z"   ; YYYYMMDDThhmmssZ

   ; -- X-FEED --

   XFEED-prop      = "X-FEED" *(";" xfeed-param) ":" absolute-URI CRLF
   xfeed-param     = language-param / tags-param
   language-param  = "LANGUAGE=" language-tag   ; BCP 47 (RFC 5646)
   tags-param      = "TAGS=" tag *("," tag)
   tag             = 1*(ALPHA / DIGIT)

   ; -- X-ENDORSE --

   XENDORSE-prop   = "X-ENDORSE" ";" "SIG=" hex-sig
                     [";" "DATE=" date-time]
                     [";" "CONFIDENCE=" confidence]
                     ";" encoding-param ":" b64-key CRLF
   hex-sig         = 128(%x30-39 / %x61-66)   ; lowercase hex, Ed25519
   confidence      = "low" / "medium" / "high"

   ; -- X-SOCIAL --

   XSOCIAL-prop    = "X-SOCIAL" ";" "PLATFORM=" platform ":"
                     social-value CRLF
   platform        = 1*(%x61-7A)              ; lowercase a-z
   social-value    = 1*value-char

   ; -- X-DSI-VERSION --

   XVERSION-prop   = "X-DSI-VERSION"
                     [";" "FEATURES=" feature *("," feature)]
                     ":" revision CRLF
   feature         = 1*(ALPHA / DIGIT / "-")
   revision        = 2DIGIT                   ; e.g. "00", "01"

   ; -- shared --

   b64-key         = 1*(ALPHA / DIGIT / "+" / "/" / "=")
   absolute-URI    = <absolute-URI, as defined in RFC 3986 Section 4.3>
   value-char      = <value-char, as defined in RFC 6350 Section 3.3>